**Polymorphism** means "many forms": the same operation behaves differently depending on the type of object or arguments. It lets code work against a general type (an interface or base class) while specific implementations supply the behaviour.

**Compile-time (static) polymorphism: method overloading.** Several methods in the same class share a name but differ in parameter list (number, type or order). The compiler picks the method from the argument types at compile time. Return type alone cannot distinguish overloads. (C++ also has operator overloading and templates; Java does not allow user-defined operator overloading.)

**Runtime (dynamic) polymorphism: method overriding.** A subclass provides its own implementation of a method with the **same signature** as one in its superclass. The method called depends on the **actual object type at runtime** (dynamic dispatch through the virtual table in C++, or virtual methods in Java).

```java
class Shape { double area() { return 0; } }
class Circle extends Shape {
    double r; Circle(double r) { this.r = r; }
    @Override double area() { return Math.PI * r * r; }
}
Shape s = new Circle(2);
System.out.println(s.area());   // Circle's area, decided at runtime

class Calc {
    int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
    double add(double a, double b) { return a + b; }   // overloading
}
```

**Overloading vs overriding.**
- **Where:** same class (or inherited) vs parent and child classes.
- **Signature:** must differ in parameters vs must be the same.
- **Binding:** compile time vs runtime.
- **Return type:** can differ vs same or covariant.
- **Access:** any vs cannot be more restrictive than the parent's.
- **Static, final and private methods** cannot be overridden (static methods are hidden, not overridden).
- Use `@Override` so the compiler checks it; in C++ the base method must be `virtual`.

**Why it matters:** extensibility (add a new shape without changing code that uses `Shape`), loose coupling, and cleaner code.
